! ElasticSolve honouring "Relative Integration Order".
!
! StressSolve has always read this keyword and passed it down to GaussPoints;
! ElasticSolve called GaussPoints bare, so the keyword was accepted by the sif
! reader and then ignored. The core does not supply it for free either -- it is
! read in GaussPointsAdapt, which ElasticSolve does not call.
!
! Unlike the system-reuse keywords beside it, this one is not behaviour-neutral,
! so a reference norm can pin it properly rather than merely check that nothing
! moved. Three RUNs: absent, explicitly zero, and reduced.
!
! It matters most for p-elements, where the default rule is whatever the element
! declares -- fem/tests/ElastPelem2dPmultg* are StressSolve cases that turn it
! down to keep a p-refined solve affordable. A plain bilinear mesh is used here
! because it isolates the keyword: the standard rule already integrates this
! exactly, so only the reduced rule moves the answer.

Header
  Mesh DB "." "square"
End

Simulation
  Coordinate System = Cartesian 2D
  Simulation Type = Steady State
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
End

Body 1
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
End

Material 1
  Youngs Modulus = 1.0e3
  Poisson Ratio = 0.3
  Density = 1.0
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers(1) = 1
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "Elasticity"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = -dofs 2 Displacement
  Large Deflection = False

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPack
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Target Boundaries(1) = 1
  Displacement 1 = Real 0.0
  Displacement 2 = Real 0.0
End

Boundary Condition 2
  Target Boundaries(1) = 3
  Force 1 = Real 5.0
  Force 2 = Real 3.0
End

! The keyword absent, which is the rule GaussPoints picks on its own.
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.70786776E-03
RUN

! Explicitly zero must be the same rule, not merely a similar one. Worth its own
! RUN because passing RelOrder at all takes a different branch of GaussPoints
! than omitting it, and the two agreeing is what makes it safe to pass the value
! unconditionally the way StressSolve does.
Solver 1 :: Relative Integration Order = Integer 0
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.70786776E-03
RUN

! Reduced integration, and the one value here that moves the answer -- by 0.65%,
! which is what shows the keyword is actually reaching the quadrature. Were it
! ignored again this RUN would report the norm above.
Solver 1 :: Relative Integration Order = Integer -1
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.71905600E-03
RUN
